## Proof

Let $\alpha = \measuredangle BAC$, $\beta = \measuredangle ABC$, $\gamma = \measuredangle ACB$, so $\alpha < \gamma$ and $\alpha+\beta+\gamma=180^\circ$. We work with directed angles modulo $180^\circ$, denoted $\measuredangle(\ell_1,\ell_2)$.

**Step 1. Angles in $\triangle ADE$.** Since $D\in AC$ and $E\in AB$, we have $\angle DAE = \alpha$. Since $E\in AB$, the angle $\angle AED = 180^\circ - \angle BED = 180^\circ - \gamma$. Hence
$$\angle ADE = 180^\circ - \alpha - (180^\circ-\gamma) = \gamma - \alpha > 0.$$

**Step 2. Translate the tangency conditions.** For a circle through $P,Q,R$, the tangent $t_P$ at $P$ satisfies the tangent–chord theorem $\measuredangle(t_P, PQ) = \measuredangle(PR, QR)$.

Write $\theta_X = \operatorname{dir}(FX)$ for the direction of line $FX$.

*Tangency 1:* the circumcircles of $BCF$ and $DEF$ are tangent at $F$ (they share $F$ and tangency must occur at the common point). Let $t$ be the common tangent at $F$. Applying the tangent–chord theorem with chords $FB$ (third point $C$) and $FE$ (third point $D$):
$$\measuredangle(t,FB)=\measuredangle(FC,BC),\qquad \measuredangle(t,FE)=\measuredangle(FD,ED).$$
Subtracting and using that the tangent is the same line,
$$\measuredangle(FE,FB)=\measuredangle(FC,BC)-\measuredangle(FD,ED).$$
In terms of directions this reads
$$\theta_B-\theta_E = (\operatorname{dir}CB-\theta_C)-(\operatorname{dir}DE-\theta_D),$$
i.e.
$$\theta_B-\theta_E+\theta_C-\theta_D = \operatorname{dir}CB-\operatorname{dir}DE. \tag{I}$$

*Tangency 2:* the circumcircles of $BEF$ and $CDF$ are tangent at $F$. With chords $FB$ (third point $E$) and $FD$ (third point $C$):
$$\measuredangle(t,FB)=\measuredangle(FE,BE),\qquad \measuredangle(t,FD)=\measuredangle(FC,DC),$$
so
$$\measuredangle(FD,FB)=\measuredangle(FE,BE)-\measuredangle(FC,DC),$$
i.e.
$$\theta_B-\theta_D+\theta_E-\theta_C = \operatorname{dir}AB-\operatorname{dir}AC. \tag{II}$$

**Step 3. Evaluate the right-hand sides.** Take $\operatorname{dir}AB=0$, so $\operatorname{dir}AC=\alpha$; thus
$$\operatorname{dir}AB-\operatorname{dir}AC = -\alpha. \tag{II$_0$}$$

For $\operatorname{dir}CB$: since $\angle ACB=\gamma$, $\operatorname{dir}CB \equiv \operatorname{dir}CA+\gamma \equiv \alpha+\gamma \pmod{180^\circ}$.

For $\operatorname{dir}DE$: in $\triangle ADE$ the angle at $D$ is $\gamma-\alpha$, so $\measuredangle(DA,DE)=\gamma-\alpha$; since $D,A,C$ are collinear, $\operatorname{dir}DA\equiv\alpha$, hence
$$\operatorname{dir}DE \equiv \alpha+(\gamma-\alpha)=\gamma \pmod{180^\circ}.$$
Therefore
$$\operatorname{dir}CB-\operatorname{dir}DE = (\alpha+\gamma)-\gamma = \alpha. \tag{I$_0$}$$

**Step 4. Conclude.** Substituting (I$_0$) and (II$_0$):
$$\theta_B-\theta_E+\theta_C-\theta_D = \alpha,\qquad \theta_B-\theta_D+\theta_E-\theta_C = -\alpha.$$
Subtracting the second from the first gives $2(\theta_C-\theta_E)=2\alpha$, i.e.
$$\measuredangle(FE,FC)=\theta_C-\theta_E=\alpha.$$

But $E\in AB$ and $\angle BAC=\alpha$, so
$$\measuredangle(AE,AC)=\alpha.$$

Thus $\measuredangle(FE,FC)=\measuredangle(AE,AC)$: the points $F$ and $A$ subtend the segment $EC$ under equal directed angles. Hence $A,C,E,F$ are concyclic. $\qquad\blacksquare$

*(Remark: adding the two equations also yields $\theta_B=\theta_D$, i.e. $B,D,F$ are collinear.)*

